{
  "image_name": "1defc50cedbe02e1d9436a8ed6754175.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several field-effect transistor–based biosensing layouts that convert events at a liquid-facing receptor surface into an electrical signal. In all cases, analyte binding or interfacial charge near the receptor layer changes the local electric field, which modulates charge carriers in an underlying semiconductor channel and is read between source and drain or as a current.\n\nThe top schematic shows a conventional electrolyte-gated transistor: a biased gate in solution induces ions in the liquid and opposite charge in the semiconductor beneath a receptor layer, controlling conduction between source and drain. The middle schematic adds optical excitation, apparently a light-addressable or photoelectrochemical configuration. A laser illuminates a defined spot through the analyte solution and layered structure, creating a depletion region and generating photocurrent in p-type silicon; a reference and working electrode measure the bias-dependent current response. The bottom schematic depicts a capacitive sensing arrangement with a sensing electrode, dielectric, electrolyte, and counter electrode, where bound analytes at membrane-like receptors alter electron flow in an external circuit.\n\nOverall, the figure compares related transduction mechanisms—electrostatic gating, photoinduced current generation, and capacitive readout—for detecting biochemical interactions at functionalized surfaces.",
